{
  "id": "strings-in-depth",
  "title": "Strings in Depth",
  "exercises": [
    {
      "id": "01",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "First Character",
      "sidebar_tag": "indexing",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a string and print its <strong>first</strong> character, using",
        "indexing.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "hello",
        "h</pre>",
        "<p>Just like a list, each character in a string has a position, starting",
        "at 0. <code>s[0]</code> gets the first character.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print the first character of s"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hello"
          ],
          "expected": "h"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Python"
          ],
          "expected": "P"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "a"
          ],
          "expected": "a"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "zebra"
          ],
          "expected": "z"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "02",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "Last Character",
      "sidebar_tag": "indexing",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a string and print its <strong>last</strong> character, using",
        "<strong>negative</strong> indexing.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "hello",
        "o</pre>",
        "<p><code>s[-1]</code> gets the last character, regardless of how long",
        "the string is &mdash; no need to know or calculate its length first.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print the last character of s"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hello"
          ],
          "expected": "o"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Python"
          ],
          "expected": "n"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "a"
          ],
          "expected": "a"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "zebra"
          ],
          "expected": "a"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "03",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "Substring with Slicing",
      "sidebar_tag": "slicing",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a string and print just its first three characters, using",
        "slicing.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "hello world",
        "hel</pre>",
        "<p><code>s[0:3]</code> takes a <strong>slice</strong> of the string from",
        "index 0 up to (but not including) index 3.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print the first three characters of s using slicing"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hello world"
          ],
          "expected": "hel"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Python"
          ],
          "expected": "Pyt"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "programming"
          ],
          "expected": "pro"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hi"
          ],
          "expected": "hi"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "04",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "String Length",
      "sidebar_tag": "len()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a string and print how many characters it has, using the",
        "built-in <code>len()</code> function.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "hello",
        "5</pre>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print the length of s"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hello"
          ],
          "expected": "5"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Python programming"
          ],
          "expected": "18"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "a"
          ],
          "expected": "1"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hi there friend"
          ],
          "expected": "15"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "05",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "Shout and Whisper",
      "sidebar_tag": "upper / lower",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a string and print it in all uppercase, then all lowercase,",
        "both on the same line separated by a space.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "Hello",
        "HELLO hello</pre>",
        "<p><code>.upper()</code> and <code>.lower()</code> return a new string",
        "&mdash; the original string itself doesn't change.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print s.upper() with end=\" \", then print s.lower()"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Hello"
          ],
          "expected": "HELLO hello"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "PYTHON"
          ],
          "expected": "PYTHON python"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "MiXeD"
          ],
          "expected": "MIXED mixed"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "06",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "Strip Whitespace",
      "sidebar_tag": "strip()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a string and print it with any leading or trailing spaces",
        "removed, using <code>.strip()</code>.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "  hello  ",
        "hello</pre>",
        "<p>This is especially useful for cleaning up text a person typed, since",
        "it's easy to accidentally leave an extra space at the start or end.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print s with leading/trailing whitespace removed"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "  hello  "
          ],
          "expected": "hello"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "no spaces"
          ],
          "expected": "no spaces"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "   leading"
          ],
          "expected": "leading"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "trailing   "
          ],
          "expected": "trailing"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "07",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "Count the Words",
      "sidebar_tag": "split()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a sentence and print how many words it contains.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "the quick brown fox",
        "4</pre>",
        "<p><code>.split()</code> breaks a string apart into a list of words",
        "wherever there's whitespace, so counting words is just a matter of",
        "counting how many items ended up in that list.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print how many words s.split() produces"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "the quick brown fox"
          ],
          "expected": "4"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hello"
          ],
          "expected": "1"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "one two"
          ],
          "expected": "2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "08",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "Loop Over Words",
      "sidebar_tag": "split() + for",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a sentence and print each word on its own line, in order.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "the quick brown",
        "the",
        "quick",
        "brown</pre>",
        "<p><code>s.split()</code> gives you back a list of words &mdash; and a",
        "<code>for</code> loop can walk through a list of strings exactly the",
        "same way it walks through a range of numbers.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: for each word in s.split(), print that word"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "the quick brown"
          ],
          "expected": "the\nquick\nbrown"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hi there"
          ],
          "expected": "hi\nthere"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "09",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "Reverse a String",
      "sidebar_tag": "slicing",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a string and print it backwards.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "hello",
        "olleh</pre>",
        "<p><code>s[::-1]</code> is a slice with a step of <code>-1</code> &mdash;",
        "walking through the string one character at a time, but",
        "<strong>backwards</strong>.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print s reversed, using slicing"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hello"
          ],
          "expected": "olleh"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Python"
          ],
          "expected": "nohtyP"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "a"
          ],
          "expected": "a"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "racecar"
          ],
          "expected": "racecar"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "Count the Vowels",
      "sidebar_tag": "for + in",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a string and count how many of its characters are vowels",
        "(<code>a</code>, <code>e</code>, <code>i</code>, <code>o</code>,",
        "<code>u</code>, upper or lowercase), then print the count.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "Hello World",
        "3</pre>",
        "<p>A <code>for</code> loop can walk through a string one character at a",
        "time, just like it walks through a list of words. The <code>in</code>",
        "operator checks whether a character shows up anywhere inside another",
        "string &mdash; <code>ch in \"aeiouAEIOU\"</code> checks whether",
        "<code>ch</code> is one of those ten letters.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "count = 0",
        "",
        "# TODO: for each character ch in s, add 1 to count if",
        "# ch in \"aeiouAEIOU\". Then print count."
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Hello World"
          ],
          "expected": "3"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "xyz"
          ],
          "expected": "0"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "AEIOU"
          ],
          "expected": "5"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Programming"
          ],
          "expected": "3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "Find and Replace",
      "sidebar_tag": "replace()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a sentence, a word to find, and a word to replace it with, and",
        "print the sentence with that replacement made.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "I have a cat",
        "cat",
        "dog",
        "I have a dog</pre>",
        "<p><code>.replace(old, new)</code> swaps out <strong>every</strong>",
        "occurrence of <code>old</code> in the string, not just the first",
        "one.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "old = input()",
        "new = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print s with every occurrence of old replaced by new"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "I have a cat",
            "cat",
            "dog"
          ],
          "expected": "I have a dog"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "banana",
            "a",
            "o"
          ],
          "expected": "bonono"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hello",
            "z",
            "x"
          ],
          "expected": "hello"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "12",
      "source_unit": "strings-in-depth",
      "title": "Fix the Bug",
      "sidebar_tag": "debug",
      "description": [
        "<p>This code is supposed to capitalize the first letter of a word by",
        "replacing it directly. Run it and see what actually happens.</p>",
        "<p>Strings in Python are <strong>immutable</strong> &mdash; once",
        "created, a string's characters can never be changed in place, even a",
        "single one. Instead of assigning to a position inside an existing",
        "string, you have to build a <strong>new</strong> string (for example,",
        "by combining slices) and assign that new string back to the",
        "variable.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "s = input()",
        "s[0] = \"H\"",
        "print(s)"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "hello"
          ],
          "expected": "Hello"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "python"
          ],
          "expected": "Hython"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "zebra"
          ],
          "expected": "Hebra"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
